API audit log
==============

Overview
========

The API audit log is pillar 4 of #43881. It records every REST API request
in a database table that administrators can filter and export, and a cron
task scans that table for anomalous activity and sends e-mail alerts.

The log is opt-in: it is disabled by default (setting
api_audit_log_enabled). While it is enabled, it covers every REST API
request, i.e. every request whose format is .json or .xml, including
anonymous requests and requests whose credential was rejected. HTML, Atom
and other non-API requests are never recorded.

Recording works through the process_action.action_controller
notification: ApplicationController#find_current_user describes the
presented credential (Redmine::ApiAudit::Credential),
ApplicationController#append_info_to_payload adds that description to
the notification payload, and Redmine::ApiAudit::Subscriber
(lib/redmine/api_audit/subscriber.rb) inserts one ApiAuditEvent per
request. Rails fills in the payload even when a filter halted the request
or an exception is propagating, so the recorded status is the final one:
401/403 from a filter, a status rendered by rescue_from, or, for a
propagating exception, the status Rails maps it to
(ActionDispatch::ExceptionWrapper), e.g. 400 for a malformed request body
and 500 otherwise.

What is recorded
================

Events
------

One row in api_audit_events (app/models/api_audit_event.rb, created by
db/migrate/20260923100000_create_api_audit_events.rb) per API request:

  created_on                time of the request
  user_id                   the effective user; with X-Redmine-Switch-User,
                            the switched-to user; empty when anonymous or
                            when authentication failed
  switched_from_user_id     the administrator who authenticated, when
                            X-Redmine-Switch-User was used
  auth_kind                 none, api_key, pat, oauth or basic (see below)
  auth_failed               true when the presented credential was rejected
  personal_access_token_id  the token that authenticated (successful pat
                            requests only)
  oauth_application_id      the OAuth application, whenever the access
                            token is known
  http_method               GET, POST, PUT, ...
  path                      request.path, scrubbed to valid UTF-8 and
                            truncated to 255 characters; never the query
                            string
  status                    the final response status (500 when Rails
                            reports none)
  ip                        request.remote_ip (up to 45 characters, so
                            IPv6 addresses fit)

There are no foreign keys: deleting a user, a personal access token or an
OAuth application leaves its events in place, and the administration
page shows it as "deleted (#id)". Rows only leave the table through
retention pruning.

Credential kinds
----------------

  none     no credential was presented, or the presented one was not
           evaluated (REST API disabled, or the action does not accept
           API authentication)
  api_key  the legacy API key, in the "key" parameter, the
           X-Redmine-API-Key header, or as the HTTP Basic username;
           failed when no active user has that key
  pat      a personal access token, by any of the same transports;
           failed when the token is unknown, expired or its owner is
           not active
  oauth    an OAuth (Doorkeeper) access token; failed when the token is
           unknown, revoked or expired, or when its owner is locked or
           deleted
  basic    HTTP Basic with a username and password; failed when the
           login fails, or when it is refused because the user has
           two-factor authentication active

Rules:

* A failed HTTP Basic attempt whose username is not shaped like a
  personal access token is recorded as basic: the request does not tell a
  wrong password from an unknown legacy key sent as the username. A
  successful legacy key sent as the Basic username is recorded as api_key.
* An unknown OAuth access token, sent in the Authorization: Bearer header
  or as the access_token or bearer_token parameter, is recorded as a
  failed oauth attempt. This check is made last, only when no other
  credential was presented, so it never changes which credential
  authenticates or the response.
* With X-Redmine-Switch-User, user_id is the switched-to user and
  switched_from_user_id the administrator whose credential was used. An
  invalid switch header (412) is not an authentication failure.
* The 403 "You must change your password" response is not an
  authentication failure: the credential was valid.
* An API request whose body cannot be parsed (e.g. malformed JSON) is
  still recorded, with its format taken from the route. Parsing fails in
  find_current_user before any credential is evaluated, so it is
  recorded as none, with no user and status 400, whatever credential
  was sent.

Never recorded
--------------

The log never contains a key or token value, a password, the query
string, request headers or the request body. A personal access token
passed as "?key=" does not appear in path, which excludes the query
string.

Settings
========

Administration -> Settings -> API, in the "API audit log" box
(config/settings.yml):

  api_audit_log_enabled               default 0 (off). Records REST API
                                      requests. The scan does nothing
                                      while it is off.
  api_audit_log_retention_days        default 90. Days to keep events
                                      and alerts.
  api_audit_alert_failures_threshold  default 10. Failed authentications
                                      from one IP address within 10
                                      minutes that trigger an alert.
  api_audit_alert_volume_threshold    default 1000. Successful requests
                                      of one user within 60 minutes that
                                      trigger an alert.
  api_audit_alert_new_ip              default 1 (on). Notifies users of
                                      API access with their credentials
                                      from a new IP address.

The three numeric settings have "format: int": a non-integer value is
silently ignored on save and the previous value is kept (the Setting
model's numericality validation, app/models/setting.rb). A retention
of 0 or less falls back to 90 days
(Redmine::ApiAudit::DEFAULT_RETENTION_DAYS); it is not "keep forever".
A threshold of 0 or less disables its detector. All five settings have
security_notifications, so changing them notifies administrators like
the other API settings.

The windows and periods are constants of Redmine::ApiAudit::Scanner
(lib/redmine/api_audit/scanner.rb), not settings: FAILURES_WINDOW 10
minutes, VOLUME_WINDOW 60 minutes, NEW_IP_LOOKBACK 24 hours,
REPEAT_INTERVAL 1 hour, DIGEST_LIMIT 20.

Administration page
===================

Administration -> API audit log (/admin/api_audit_events,
AdminApiAuditEventsController) lists the events through ApiAuditEventQuery,
a regular Redmine query:

* default filter: created in the last 7 days; default sort: newest
  first;
* filters: date, user, credential kind, authentication failed, personal
  access token (id), status, HTTP method, IP address and path;
* default columns: date, user, credential kind, authentication failed,
  HTTP method, path, status and IP address; optional columns: switched
  from, personal access token (name and masked value) and OAuth
  application. A user, token or application that no longer exists is
  shown as "deleted (#id)";
* saved queries work as for the other administration lists (users,
  projects): they are listed in the sidebar and open on this page;
* CSV export of the filtered list, capped at the first 50,000 rows in
  the current sort order, i.e. the newest 50,000 with the default sort
  (AdminApiAuditEventsController::CSV_EXPORT_LIMIT). The export dialog
  states the cap.

The page is admin only and read only: it has no create, edit or delete
action. It shows a warning while api_audit_log_enabled is off. The menu
item is shown while the REST API is enabled, like the personal access
token and OAuth application items; the page itself is reachable by
administrators regardless of that setting.

Cron tasks
==========

Two rake tasks (lib/tasks/redmine.rake) must run from cron:

  redmine:api_audit:scan   runs the detectors and sends the mails
  redmine:api_audit:prune  deletes events and alerts older than the
                           retention period

Example crontab, with the scan every 5 minutes (recommended; run it at
least every 10 minutes, the failures window):

  */5 * * * * cd /path/to/redmine && flock -n tmp/api_audit_scan.lock \
    bundle exec rake redmine:api_audit:scan RAILS_ENV=production
  30 3 * * *  cd /path/to/redmine && \
    bundle exec rake redmine:api_audit:prune RAILS_ENV=production

(Crontab entries must be on one line; they are wrapped here.) "flock -n"
skips a run while the previous one is still going, so slow runs cannot
overlap. It only guards one host: when Redmine runs on several
application hosts, schedule the scan on one of them only.

The scan sends its mails synchronously (Mailer.with_synched_deliveries,
like redmine:send_reminders), so no mail is lost with the :async queue
adapter. It exits with a non-zero status when a detector or a mail
delivery failed; the errors are in the Rails log.

The prune deletes in batches of 10,000 rows by id range
(ApiAuditEvent::PRUNE_BATCH_SIZE), so that no DELETE exceeds the bind
parameter limit of any supported database (notably SQL Server).

Anomaly notifications
=====================

Redmine::ApiAudit::Scanner runs three detectors over the recorded events:

  failures  at least api_audit_alert_failures_threshold events with
            auth_failed from one IP address in the last 10 minutes.
            Recipients: active administrators (digest). Repeated for the
            same IP at most once per hour.
  volume    at least api_audit_alert_volume_threshold successful events
            of one user (user_id, the effective user) in the last 60
            minutes. Recipients: active administrators (digest).
            Repeated for the same user at most once per hour.
  new_ip    a successful request with a credential (auth_kind other than
            none) in the last 24 hours, from an IP address not seen
            for the credential's owner in the retained log (no earlier
            successful request of the owner from it), while the owner
            does have earlier successful requests. The owner is
            switched_from_user_id when present, else user_id.
            Recipient: the owner, if active. Sent once per (owner, IP)
            while the alert row exists, i.e. within the retention
            period.

Administrator digest (Mailer#api_audit_digest): the failures and volume
findings of one run are sent as ONE mail per active administrator, not
one mail per finding, so that an attacker rotating IP addresses cannot
flood the administrators or the mail relay. The digest lists at most 20
findings (DIGEST_LIMIT), the largest counts first, followed by "and N
more" when there are others. It has one section for failed
authentications per IP address and one for requests per user, each with
a link to the audit log filtered to the last day. The findings, including
the ones not listed, are recorded in api_audit_alerts only if at least
one administrator's delivery succeeded, or if there is no active
administrator; otherwise they are sent again on the next run.

New-IP mail (Mailer#api_audit_new_ip): tells the owner the time, IP
address, credential (the token name and masked value for a personal
access token) and request. Its advice and link depend on the credential
kind:

  pat      delete the personal access token (My account -> Personal
           access tokens)
  api_key  reset the API access key (My account)
  oauth    revoke the application's access (authorized applications)
  basic    change the password

The alert is recorded only when the delivery succeeded; a failed
delivery is attempted again on the next run. Owners with no earlier
successful request are skipped, otherwise every first use of the API
would be reported.

Both mails are rendered in the recipient's language.

Idempotency: there is no watermark. Every run looks back over the
sliding windows ending at the time of the run (the 10 minutes before it
for failures, the 60 minutes before it for volume, the 24 hours before it
for new IP addresses), and api_audit_alerts (created by
db/migrate/20260923100001_create_api_audit_alerts.rb) suppresses repeats.
Running the scan again sends no duplicate. A burst is only detected if a
scan runs while it is still inside the window: a missed run is covered by
the next one only when the gap is shorter than the window. One failing
detector or delivery is logged and the other detectors still run.

Security model
==============

1. Detectors only read the log after the fact. They never block, delay
   or alter a request, and never return 429. There are no counters in
   the cache or on the request path: this is not rate limiting.
2. API responses are identical whether the log and the alerts are
   enabled or not. Authentication outcomes do not change.
3. Neither collecting nor writing the audit data can break or alter the
   response. The capture hook (append_info_to_payload) runs in the
   ensure clause of process_action, so an exception there would replace
   the response: the hook catches any StandardError, logs "API audit
   data could not be collected: <class>: <message>", and skips the
   event. When request.remote_ip raises IpSpoofAttackError (Client-Ip
   and X-Forwarded-For disagree), the event is recorded with the peer
   address (REMOTE_ADDR) instead. A failed audit write is caught in the
   subscriber, logged with its error class and message (never the
   payload), and ignored.
4. The log, the administration page, the CSV export and the mails contain
   no secret: no key or token value, password, query string, header or
   body.
5. The page and the export are for administrators only. Client-supplied
   values (path, IP address) are HTML-escaped on the page and in the
   mails. In the CSV, path always starts with "/" and ip is parsed by
   Rails, so neither can start a spreadsheet formula.

Backward compatibility
======================

The log is off by default. While it is off, the request path only builds
the credential description; nothing is written. The feature adds two new
tables (api_audit_events and api_audit_alerts, both with reversible
migrations) and changes no existing table. Authentication outcomes for
legacy API keys, OAuth, HTTP Basic and personal access tokens do not
change.

Limitations
===========

* One INSERT per API request while the log is enabled.
* Requests rejected before reaching a controller (routing 404s for
  unknown URLs) are not recorded.
* Alerts depend on cron: they are only as timely as the scan interval,
  and no alert is sent while the scan does not run.
* A cron outage longer than 24 hours (NEW_IP_LOOKBACK) can miss new-IP
  alerts for that period.
* Each scan groups all successful credentialed events of the last 24
  hours by (credential owner, IP) for the new-IP detector, and runs one
  or two indexed EXISTS queries per pair not yet alerted (up to four
  when the IP is new to its owner). The scan cost therefore grows with
  the daily API traffic and the number of distinct (owner, IP) pairs.
* Two scans started at the same instant could both pass the dedup check
  before either records its alert, and both send it. "flock -n" on a
  single host avoids this (see "Cron tasks").
* With production's default "raise_delivery_errors = false"
  (config/environments/production.rb), delivery errors of any delivery
  method are caught and only logged by Redmine's Mailer.deliver_mail
  ("Email delivery error: ..."): the scan does not see them, so the
  retry on the next run and the non-zero exit status do not happen.
* Anonymous request volume is visible in the log but has no detector:
  the volume detector counts per user.
* The volume detector counts per effective user: requests made with
  X-Redmine-Switch-User count for the switched-to user, not for the
  administrator whose credential was used.
* In the CSV export, text columns other than path and ip (user name,
  personal access token name, OAuth application name) are exported like
  any other Redmine CSV export, without spreadsheet formula escaping.
* Sorting the administration list on an unindexed column (e.g. path or
  status) without a date filter is slow on a very large table; the
  default 7-day filter avoids it.
* Only English strings were added (config/locales/en.yml).

Known issues found in existing code (pre-existing, out of scope)
==================================================================

These issues were found while building this feature, in code it touches
but did not introduce. They are left unfixed:

* An OAuth access token that is accessible but whose owner is locked or
  deleted makes ApplicationController#find_current_user raise a
  NoMethodError (User.active.find_by_id returns nil, then oauth_scope= is
  called on it), i.e. a 500 response. This is unchanged; the audit log
  records the request as a failed oauth attempt with status 500.
* QueriesController#new and #create do not restrict the admin-only query
  types (UserQuery, ProjectAdminQuery, ApiAuditEventQuery): a non-admin
  with the "Save queries" permission (checked by authorize_global) can
  save a private query row of these types. Such a row appears in every
  administrator's sidebar, because ApiAuditEventQuery.visible returns
  every query for administrators, the same pattern as for UserQuery and
  ProjectAdminQuery. For ApiAuditEventQuery the user filter values are
  hidden from non-admins, and no audit data is exposed: the list only
  runs on the admin-only page.
* Rails::Rack::Logger logs request.remote_ip at the info level, before
  Redmine runs and outside ActionDispatch::ShowExceptions. With a log
  level of info or lower (Redmine's default is debug), a request whose
  Client-Ip and X-Forwarded-For headers disagree raises
  IpSpoofAttackError there and fails in the application server, whether
  or not the audit log is enabled.

